# Snapshot tests (SchemaLockTest, SymbolTableSnapshotTest) compare
# byte-for-byte against LF-terminated serializer output. Force LF on
# the tracked snapshot + lock resource files so byte-for-byte comparison
# works identically on Windows and Linux clones regardless of the
# user's git config core.autocrlf setting.
rune-parser/src/test/resources/symbols/schema-lock.txt     text eol=lf
rune-parser/src/test/resources/symbols/snapshots/*.txt     text eol=lf

# Frozen 9.83.0 baseline corpus manifest — Corpus983BaselineManifestTest compares
# the working test-corpus/ against this committed SHA-256 contract. Pin LF so the
# manifest is byte-stable across Windows and Linux regardless of core.autocrlf.
rune-parser/src/test/resources/corpus-baseline-9.83.manifest text eol=lf

# Jazzer crash reproducers are raw bytes — treat as binary so git does
# not mangle line endings and every reproducer replays byte-identically
# on Windows devs and Linux CI. Paths follow the jazzer-junit convention
# (H20 / H22).
rune-parser/src/test/resources/com/regnosys/rosetta/harness/fuzz/**/* binary
rune-parser/crash-* binary

# Byte-diff catalogue (P1.3.3): index.json is byte-compared by CI's
# index-regen-check job (diff -u against fresh regeneration). Per-entry
# JSON files are schema-validated + emitted into the index in fixed
# Python json.dump format. Force LF on every catalogue JSON so a Windows
# checkout regenerating the index produces identical bytes to a Linux
# CI runner — same precedent as the schema-lock entries above.
docs/byte-diff-catalogue/**/*.json text eol=lf

# Byte-diff catalogue scripts and fixtures: the .test smoke tests use
# bash heredocs and Python json.dump output that are LF-terminated;
# CRLF on Windows checkouts would fail the smoke tests and the
# `index-regen-check` CI job. Pin LF.
scripts/byte-diff/**/*.py text eol=lf
scripts/byte-diff/**/*.sh text eol=lf
scripts/byte-diff/**/*.test text eol=lf
scripts/byte-diff/fixtures/*.xml text eol=lf
scripts/byte-diff/fixtures/*.json text eol=lf

# Chart generators + their SVGs: pin LF so the regen scripts produce byte-stable
# output on Windows and Linux regardless of core.autocrlf (same precedent as the
# byte-diff catalogue pins above) — the regen-x2 sha-compare discipline depends
# on it. The v3 pair added at PR #543 (the Seat-1 SF1 there also backfilled the
# regen-ir-svgs trio, unpinned since #476).
docs/assets/ir-progress.svg text eol=lf
# the v3.2 phase chart's pair (PR #635, the v3.2.0 co-review - round 30's cq SF-4 backfilled the pin)

# The IR charts' regenerator gate + its .test (PR #632 round 3, committed under scripts/ci/): bash with a quoted
# heredoc - pin LF like the byte-diff .sh/.test pins above (PR #632 round 4, cq NIT-5). Measured first: this box's MSYS
# bash runs a CRLF script with a quoted heredoc, and the two CRLF-checked-out peers in scripts/ci/ are green in every
# receipt - the pin protects a WSL / Linux bash over a Windows checkout and the .test's byte-exact sed copy.

# Resolution-conformance suite (v3.1 C1): the committed oracle dump records
# CHARACTER OFFSETS into the .rosetta snippets, so the snippets' on-disk line
# endings are part of the instrument — a CRLF checkout shifts every offset and
# the "dump regenerates byte-identical" gate silently fails on the next
# machine (found live 2026-08-16: the post-#566 checkout materialized the six
# snippets as CRLF and every regenerated offset moved). Pin LF on the
# snippets AND the dump — same precedent as the schema-lock entries above.
rune-parser/src/test/resources/resolution-conformance/*.rosetta text eol=lf
rune-parser/src/test/resources/resolution-conformance/*.tsv text eol=lf

# rune-runtime = the verbatim released 9.83.0 source port (U018). The three
# classpath resources are byte-locked (SHA-256, RuntimeSurfaceLockTest)
# against the released binary jar and served through the maven plugin's
# builtin-model channel — pin LF so a fresh checkout keeps the released
# bytes regardless of core.autocrlf; sources stay LF for verbatim
# diffability against the released sources jar.
rune-runtime/src/main/resources/** text eol=lf
rune-runtime/src/main/java/**/*.java text eol=lf
rune-runtime/src/test/java/**/*.java text eol=lf
rune-runtime/pom.xml text eol=lf

# The C2a evidence-API ledger + its gate (v3.1 C2): the ledger's snippet keys
# are whitespace-normalised (CRLF-immune by design) but the instrument family
# is LF-pinned on principle after the #567 lesson — a committed instrument's
# line endings are part of the instrument.
scripts/ci/evidence-api-ledger.py text eol=lf
scripts/ci/evidence-api-ledger.py.test text eol=lf
scripts/ci/evidence-api-ledger.tsv text eol=lf
# The C2d triage instrument (PR #608) is the ledger's sibling and takes the same pin (PR #609).

# The v3.2 chaos declared sets are committed instruments: their line endings are
# part of the instrument (the #567 lesson). The cell's own `** -text` covers the
# in-cell register; these are the module-resource ones.
rune-parser/src/test/resources/resolution-conformance/chaos-l2-expected-differences.txt text eol=lf
rune-java-generator/src/test/resources/chaos-expected-divergence.txt text eol=lf
rune-java-generator/src/test/resources/chaos-golden-free.txt text eol=lf
rune-ir-java-optimised/src/test/resources/chaos-optimised-route-broken.txt text eol=lf

# The maven plugin's route fixture (v3.3 seat 9, PR #645 commit 14b): RunePluginRunnerRouteTest
# compares the runner's generated output against these goldens BYTE-FOR-BYTE on all three routes,
# so their line endings ARE the instrument (the #567 lesson, the holdout goldens' normalising
# compare deliberately not copied - a byte gate that normalises is not a byte gate). The generator
# emits LF; pin LF on the goldens AND on the .rosetta source that feeds them, so a core.autocrlf
# checkout on Windows compares the same bytes a Linux one does.
rune-maven-plugin/src/test/resources/route-fixture/** text eol=lf

# Compact evidence files: their SHA-256 values are recorded in docs/evidence/inventory.json, so Git must never convert their line endings.
docs/evidence/** -text
